--TEST--
`Core\Request::json()` reads the request body as one JSON document, carrying `Core\Json::decode`'s `{maxDepth?}` bag and consulting no `Content-Type` `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`
--HEADERS--
Content-Type: text/plain
--POST_RAW--
{"name":"ada","tags":[1,2],"ok":true}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The member is `Core\Json::decode` over the body's octets and nothing else,
// so the document it answers re-encodes to what the peer sent.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::json()), "\n";

// The peer declared `text/plain` and the body was read as JSON regardless:
// what a peer wrote in a header is not what decides what a body is, which is
// `post()`'s rule already. A member that gated on the type would have thrown
// above rather than printing the document.
echo "the declared type is not consulted\n";

// The bag is `decode`'s own, counted `decode`'s way: a scalar document is
// depth 1, so the list under `tags` sits at 3 and a cap of 2 refuses this
// document. The refusal is a `ParseError`, the same class a malformed body
// raises, because both are the peer's document failing to be what it must be.
try {
    Core\Request::json({maxDepth: 2});
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $deep) {
    echo "too deep\n";
}

// And the cap is the call's, not the request's: the octets are still the ones
// the peer sent, so the same body at the default depth answers as before.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::json()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"name":"ada","tags":[1,2],"ok":true}
the declared type is not consulted
too deep
{"name":"ada","tags":[1,2],"ok":true}
